module

public import Mathlib.RingTheory.FiniteType
public import StacksCore.Tactic.StacksAttribute
public import StacksFrontier.RingTheory.DividedPowers.TateResolution.FiniteTateTower
public import StacksFrontier.RingTheory.DividedPowers.TateResolution.TateColimitResolution

/-! # Tate's resolution with finitely many generators in each degree

`StacksFrontier/RingTheory/DividedPowers/TateResolution/TateColimitResolution.lean` assembles the
*existence* half of Tag 09PN: every surjection `φ : R[xᵢ] → S` from a polynomial ring extends to a
Tate resolution. Its tower adjoins, in degree `n + 1`, one variable for **every** cycle of degree
`n`, so the resolution it produces is not degreewise finite.

This file assembles the last sentence of Tag 09PN — *if `R` is Noetherian and `R → S` is of finite
type, then `A` can be taken to have only finitely many generators in each degree* — out of the
degreewise finite tower of
`StacksFrontier/RingTheory/DividedPowers/TateResolution/FiniteTateTower.lean`. The construction is the
same as for the existence half and the four conditions are checked in the same way; the two
differences are that the base is `R[x₁, …, x_k]` with a **finite** generating family of `ker φ` as
the family of degree-zero cycles to be killed, and that exactness in each degree comes from
`DividedPowers.TateResolution.exists_finiteTateColimitDifferential_boundary_of_cycle`, which needs
the chosen families really to span — which over a Noetherian coefficient ring they do, by
`DividedPowers.TateResolution.hasFiniteSpanningCycles_finiteTateStage`.

The augmentation and its kernel are handled exactly as for the existence half, by the subring
trick: the elements all of whose homogeneous components have their boundary killed by the
augmentation form a subring — closure under multiplication being the Leibniz rule, with the Koszul
sign harmless because a factor of positive degree is killed outright — and that subring contains
the coefficients, the variables and all their divided powers, hence the whole colimit.

## Main definitions

* `DividedPowers.TateResolution.Resolution.polynomialIndices` — the index type of the variables of
  degree `i + 1` of a Tate resolution, with the resolution's own instances supplied. This is what
  "finitely many generators in each degree" is a statement about.
* `DividedPowers.TateResolution.finiteResolutionBase` — the base of the degreewise finite tower
  resolving `φ`: the polynomial ring graded in degree zero, with a finite generating family of
  `ker φ` as the family of cycles to be killed.
* `DividedPowers.TateResolution.finiteResolutionAlgebra`,
  `DividedPowers.TateResolution.finiteResolutionDifferential`,
  `DividedPowers.TateResolution.finiteResolutionCoefficient`,
  `DividedPowers.TateResolution.finiteResolutionVariable` and
  `DividedPowers.TateResolution.finiteResolutionAugmentation` — the carrier of the resolution with
  its differential, its degree-zero polynomial algebra, its variables and its augmentation.
* `DividedPowers.TateResolution.finiteResolutionPolynomial` — the resolution as a graded divided
  power polynomial algebra over `R[xᵢ]`.
* `DividedPowers.TateResolution.finiteTateResolution` — the resolution itself.

## Main results

* `DividedPowers.TateResolution.finiteResolutionAugmentation_differential` — the augmentation kills
  every boundary.
* `DividedPowers.TateResolution.finiteTateResolution` — a surjection from a polynomial ring whose
  kernel is generated by a prescribed finite family extends to a Tate resolution with finitely many
  variables in each degree, provided the polynomial ring is Noetherian.
* `DividedPowers.TateResolution.exists_finite_tateResolution` — the finiteness half of Tag 09PN: a
  Noetherian `R` and an `R`-algebra `S` of finite type admit a Tate resolution with finitely many
  generators in each degree.

## Implementation notes

The index type of the variables of a presentation lies in the universe of the carrier, so the
variables of degree one are indexed by a subtype of the moved copy
`DividedPowers.TateResolution.resolutionCoefficientRing` of `R[xᵢ]` rather than of `R[xᵢ]` itself.
In `exists_finite_tateResolution` the variables of the presentation are indexed by `Fin k`, which
`DividedPowers.TateResolution.resolutionCoefficientRing` then moves into the universe of the
resolution; no `ULift` is visible in the statement.

Noetherianness is required of the *coefficient* ring `R[xᵢ]` rather than of `R`, which is what the
tower's finiteness argument uses; `exists_finite_tateResolution` discharges it from
`IsNoetherianRing R` and finiteness of the variable set by `MvPolynomial.isNoetherianRing`.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 09PN](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/09PN)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 09PM](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/09PM)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0F4I](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0F4I)
-/

@[expose] public section

namespace DividedPowers.TateResolution

universe u v w

attribute [local instance] GradedDPAlgebra.ring GradedDPAlgebra.algebra
  GradedDPAlgebra.graded GradedDPAlgebra.strict

attribute [local instance] gradedLimitGradedAlgebra gradedLimitStrictlyGradedComm

attribute [local instance] TateDatum.dga TateDatum.compatible

/-- **The index type of the variables of degree `i + 1` of a Tate resolution.** The resolution
carries its ring, algebra, grading and graded-commutativity as *fields*, so the presentation it
also carries can only be projected with those supplied; this definition supplies them, and is what
the finiteness assertion of Tag 09PN — finitely many generators in each degree — is a statement
about. -/
public def Resolution.polynomialIndices {R : Type u} {S : Type v} {σ : Type w}
    [CommRing R] [CommRing S] [Algebra R S] {φ : MvPolynomial σ R →ₐ[R] S}
    (A : Resolution R S σ φ) (i : ℕ) : Type (max u v w) :=
  letI : Ring A.A := A.ring
  letI : Algebra R A.A := A.algebra
  letI : GradedAlgebra A.grade := A.graded
  letI : IsStrictlyGradedComm A.grade := A.strict
  A.polynomial.indices i

section Resolution

variable {R : Type u} {S : Type v} {σ : Type w} [CommRing R]
  (s₀ : Finset (resolutionCoefficientRing R S σ))

/-- **The base of the degreewise finite tower resolving `φ`**: the polynomial ring `R[xᵢ]` graded
in degree zero and carrying the zero differential, with the prescribed finite family `s₀` as the
family of degree-zero cycles to be killed by the variables of degree one.

Where the existence half of Tag 09PN kills the whole of `ker φ` — one variable of degree one for
each of its elements — this kills a finite generating family of it, which is what the Stacks source
does. Only the family matters, not which surjection it generates the kernel of, so the presentation
is not a parameter here; it reappears with the augmentation. -/
public noncomputable abbrev finiteResolutionBase : TateStageData.{u, max v w} R 0 :=
  finiteTateBase.{u, max v w} (R := R) (resolutionCoefficientRing R S σ) s₀

/-- **The carrier of the degreewise finite Tate resolution**: the colimit of the tower over the
base. -/
public noncomputable abbrev finiteResolutionAlgebra : GradedDPAlgebra.{u, max u v w} R :=
  finiteTateColimit (finiteResolutionBase s₀)

/-- The differential of the degreewise finite Tate resolution. -/
public noncomputable abbrev finiteResolutionDifferential :
    (finiteResolutionAlgebra s₀).carrier →ₗ[R] (finiteResolutionAlgebra s₀).carrier :=
  finiteTateColimitDifferential (finiteResolutionBase s₀)

/-- The degree-zero polynomial algebra of the resolution: the inclusion of the base of the tower,
read through the identification of that base with `R[xᵢ]`. -/
public noncomputable def finiteResolutionCoefficient :
    MvPolynomial σ R →ₐ[R] (finiteResolutionAlgebra s₀).carrier :=
  (finiteTateCoefficient (finiteResolutionBase s₀)).hom.comp
    (resolutionCoefficientEquiv R S σ).symm.toAlgHom

/-- The variables of the resolution, indexed together with their degrees. -/
public noncomputable def finiteResolutionVariable
    (j : Σ n : ℕ, finiteTateIndex (finiteResolutionBase s₀) n) :
    (finiteResolutionAlgebra s₀).carrier :=
  finiteTateVariable (finiteResolutionBase s₀) j.1 j.2

/-- Each variable of the resolution is homogeneous of its prescribed degree. -/
public theorem finiteResolutionVariable_mem
    (j : Σ n : ℕ, finiteTateIndex (finiteResolutionBase s₀) n) :
    finiteResolutionVariable s₀ j ∈ (finiteResolutionAlgebra s₀).grade (j.1 + 1) :=
  finiteTateVariable_mem (finiteResolutionBase s₀) j.1 j.2

/-- The degree-zero polynomial algebra of the resolution lands in degree zero. -/
public theorem finiteResolutionCoefficient_mem_grade_zero (p : MvPolynomial σ R) :
    finiteResolutionCoefficient s₀ p ∈ (finiteResolutionAlgebra s₀).grade 0 :=
  (finiteTateCoefficient (finiteResolutionBase s₀)).map_grade 0 _
    (mem_degreeZeroGrade_zero R (resolutionCoefficientRing R S σ) _)

/-- The degree-zero polynomial algebra of the resolution is a subalgebra: the inclusion of the base
of the tower is injective, and so is the identification of that base with `R[xᵢ]`. -/
public theorem finiteResolutionCoefficient_injective :
    Function.Injective (finiteResolutionCoefficient s₀) :=
  (finiteTateInclusion_injective (finiteResolutionBase s₀) 0).comp
    (resolutionCoefficientEquiv R S σ).symm.injective

/-- The image of the degree-zero polynomial algebra is the image of the base of the tower: the two
differ by an isomorphism of the base. -/
public theorem range_finiteResolutionCoefficient :
    Set.range (finiteResolutionCoefficient s₀) =
      Set.range (finiteTateCoefficient (finiteResolutionBase s₀)).hom :=
  (resolutionCoefficientEquiv R S σ).symm.surjective.range_comp
    (finiteTateCoefficient (finiteResolutionBase s₀)).hom

/-- The differential of the resolution vanishes in degree zero. -/
public theorem finiteResolutionDifferential_grade_zero
    {x : (finiteResolutionAlgebra s₀).carrier}
    (hx : x ∈ (finiteResolutionAlgebra s₀).grade 0) :
    finiteResolutionDifferential s₀ x = 0 :=
  IsDifferentialGradedAlgebra.d_grade_zero (𝒜 := (finiteResolutionAlgebra s₀).grade)
    (d := finiteResolutionDifferential s₀)
    (self := finiteTateColimitDga (finiteResolutionBase s₀)) hx

/-- The differential of the resolution satisfies the signed Leibniz rule. -/
public theorem finiteResolutionDifferential_leibniz {i k : ℕ}
    {x y : (finiteResolutionAlgebra s₀).carrier}
    (hx : x ∈ (finiteResolutionAlgebra s₀).grade i)
    (hy : y ∈ (finiteResolutionAlgebra s₀).grade k) :
    finiteResolutionDifferential s₀ (x * y) =
      finiteResolutionDifferential s₀ x * y +
        (-1 : R) ^ i • (x * finiteResolutionDifferential s₀ y) :=
  IsDifferentialGradedAlgebra.leibniz (𝒜 := (finiteResolutionAlgebra s₀).grade)
    (d := finiteResolutionDifferential s₀)
    (self := finiteTateColimitDga (finiteResolutionBase s₀)) hx hy

/-- **The degree-zero part of the resolution is the prescribed polynomial algebra.** No step of the
tower adds anything in degree zero, and the base has nothing in positive degree. -/
public theorem mem_finiteResolutionAlgebra_grade_zero_iff
    (a : (finiteResolutionAlgebra s₀).carrier) :
    a ∈ (finiteResolutionAlgebra s₀).grade 0 ↔
      ∃ p : MvPolynomial σ R, finiteResolutionCoefficient s₀ p = a := by
  rw [← Set.mem_range, range_finiteResolutionCoefficient]
  exact mem_finiteTateColimit_grade_zero_iff (R := R) (resolutionCoefficientRing R S σ) s₀ a

/-- The finite variable stages of the resolution's variables cover the resolution. -/
public theorem finiteResolutionAlgebra_finite_support (x : (finiteResolutionAlgebra s₀).carrier) :
    ∃ T : Finset (Σ n : ℕ, finiteTateIndex (finiteResolutionBase s₀) n),
      x ∈ finiteVariableStage (finiteResolutionAlgebra s₀).gamma
        (finiteResolutionCoefficient s₀) (finiteResolutionVariable s₀) T := by
  obtain ⟨T, hT⟩ := finiteTateColimit_finite_support (finiteResolutionBase s₀) x
  refine ⟨T, ?_⟩
  rwa [finiteVariableStage_congr_coefficient (finiteResolutionAlgebra s₀).gamma
    (finiteTateCoefficient (finiteResolutionBase s₀)).hom (finiteResolutionVariable s₀)
    (finiteResolutionCoefficient s₀) (range_finiteResolutionCoefficient s₀) T]

/-- **The resolution is free on its coefficients and its variables.** This is the mapping property
of the colimit of the tower, with the coefficient morphism read through the identification of the
base with `R[xᵢ]`. -/
public theorem finiteResolutionAlgebra_hasVariableMappingProperty :
    HasVariableMappingProperty (finiteResolutionAlgebra s₀).gamma
      (finiteResolutionCoefficient s₀) (finiteResolutionVariable s₀) := by
  intro E f hf t ht
  obtain ⟨F, hF1, hF2⟩ := finiteTateColimit_exists_gradedDPHom (finiteResolutionBase s₀) E
    (degreeZeroDPHom R (resolutionCoefficientRing R S σ) E
      (f.comp (resolutionCoefficientEquiv R S σ).toAlgHom) (fun p => hf _))
    (fun n j => t ⟨n, j⟩) (fun n j => ht ⟨n, j⟩)
  exact ⟨F.hom.toRingHom,
    fun p => (hF1 _).trans (congrArg f ((resolutionCoefficientEquiv R S σ).apply_symm_apply p)),
    fun j => hF2 j.1 j.2, F.map_grade, F.map_dpow⟩

/-- The resolution has degree-zero coefficients and homogeneous variables. -/
public instance finiteResolutionIsHomogeneousVariableFamily :
    IsHomogeneousVariableFamily (finiteResolutionAlgebra s₀).grade
      (finiteResolutionCoefficient s₀) (finiteResolutionVariable s₀) where
  coefficient_mem := finiteResolutionCoefficient_mem_grade_zero s₀
  variable_homogeneous j := ⟨j.1 + 1, finiteResolutionVariable_mem s₀ j⟩

/-- **The resolution is a graded divided power polynomial algebra over `R[xᵢ]`**, on the variables
adjoined by the tower. This is condition (4) of Tag 09PN, and what both freeness of the homogeneous
pieces and the finiteness assertion are read off. -/
public noncomputable def finiteResolutionPolynomial :
    GradedDividedPowerPolynomial (finiteResolutionAlgebra s₀).grade
      (finiteResolutionAlgebra s₀).gamma (finiteResolutionCoefficient s₀) :=
  gradedDividedPowerPolynomialOfMappingProperty (finiteResolutionAlgebra s₀).gamma
    (finiteResolutionCoefficient s₀) (finiteResolutionVariable s₀)
    (finiteResolutionVariable_mem s₀) (finiteResolutionCoefficient_injective s₀)
    (finiteResolutionAlgebra_finite_support s₀)
    (finiteResolutionAlgebra_hasVariableMappingProperty s₀)

/-- The variables of the presentation of the resolution are the variables adjoined by the tower, so
there are finitely many of them in each degree. -/
public theorem finiteResolutionPolynomial_indices :
    (finiteResolutionPolynomial s₀).indices = finiteTateIndex (finiteResolutionBase s₀) :=
  rfl

end Resolution

section Augmentation

variable {R : Type u} {S : Type v} {σ : Type w} [CommRing R] [CommRing S] [Algebra R S]
  (φ : MvPolynomial σ R →ₐ[R] S) (s₀ : Finset (resolutionCoefficientRing R S σ))
  (hs₀ : ∀ p ∈ s₀, resolutionPresentation φ p = 0)

/-- **The augmentation of the resolution exists**: the morphism of graded divided power algebras
out of the colimit which restricts to the prescribed presentation on the coefficients and kills
every variable, the target being `S` concentrated in degree zero. -/
public theorem exists_finiteResolutionAugmentation :
    ∃ F : GradedDPHom (finiteResolutionAlgebra s₀) (degreeZeroDPAlgebra R S),
      (∀ p : resolutionCoefficientRing R S σ,
          F.hom ((finiteTateCoefficient (finiteResolutionBase s₀)).hom p) =
            resolutionPresentation φ p) ∧
        (∀ (n : ℕ) (j : finiteTateIndex (finiteResolutionBase s₀) n),
          F.hom (finiteTateVariable (finiteResolutionBase s₀) n j) = 0) :=
  finiteTateColimit_exists_gradedDPHom (finiteResolutionBase s₀) (degreeZeroDPAlgebra R S)
    (degreeZeroDPHom R (resolutionCoefficientRing R S σ) (degreeZeroDPAlgebra R S)
      (show resolutionCoefficientRing R S σ →ₐ[R] S from resolutionPresentation φ)
      (fun _ => mem_degreeZeroGrade_zero R S _))
    (fun _ _ => 0) (fun _ _ => Submodule.zero_mem _)

/-- **The augmentation of the resolution of `φ`**: the morphism to `S` restricting to `φ` on the
degree-zero polynomial algebra and killing every variable. -/
public noncomputable def finiteResolutionAugmentation :
    GradedDPHom (finiteResolutionAlgebra s₀) (degreeZeroDPAlgebra R S) :=
  (exists_finiteResolutionAugmentation φ s₀).choose

/-- The augmentation restricts to the prescribed presentation on the base of the tower. -/
public theorem finiteResolutionAugmentation_base (p : resolutionCoefficientRing R S σ) :
    (finiteResolutionAugmentation φ s₀).hom
        ((finiteTateCoefficient (finiteResolutionBase s₀)).hom p) =
      resolutionPresentation φ p :=
  (exists_finiteResolutionAugmentation φ s₀).choose_spec.1 p

/-- The augmentation kills every variable of the resolution. -/
public theorem finiteResolutionAugmentation_variable
    (j : Σ n : ℕ, finiteTateIndex (finiteResolutionBase s₀) n) :
    (finiteResolutionAugmentation φ s₀).hom (finiteResolutionVariable s₀ j) = 0 :=
  (exists_finiteResolutionAugmentation φ s₀).choose_spec.2 j.1 j.2

/-- **The augmentation kills the positive-degree part**: its target is concentrated in degree
zero. -/
public theorem finiteResolutionAugmentation_positive {n : ℕ} (hn : n ≠ 0)
    {a : (finiteResolutionAlgebra s₀).carrier}
    (ha : a ∈ (finiteResolutionAlgebra s₀).grade n) :
    (finiteResolutionAugmentation φ s₀).hom a = 0 :=
  eq_zero_of_mem_degreeZeroDPAlgebra_grade R S hn
    ((finiteResolutionAugmentation φ s₀).map_grade n a ha)

/-- The augmentation extends the prescribed presentation. -/
public theorem finiteResolutionAugmentation_coefficient (p : MvPolynomial σ R) :
    (finiteResolutionAugmentation φ s₀).hom (finiteResolutionCoefficient s₀ p) = φ p :=
  (finiteResolutionAugmentation_base φ s₀ _).trans
    (congrArg φ ((resolutionCoefficientEquiv R S σ).apply_symm_apply p))

include hs₀ in
/-- **The augmentation kills the boundary of every variable.** For a variable of degree one the
boundary is, by construction, the member of the prescribed family indexing the variable, which lies
in `ker φ`; for a variable of higher degree it is homogeneous of positive degree, where the
augmentation vanishes. -/
public theorem finiteResolutionAugmentation_differential_variable
    (j : Σ n : ℕ, finiteTateIndex (finiteResolutionBase s₀) n) :
    (finiteResolutionAugmentation φ s₀).hom
      (finiteResolutionDifferential s₀ (finiteResolutionVariable s₀ j)) = 0 := by
  obtain ⟨n, j⟩ := j
  have hD : finiteResolutionDifferential s₀ (finiteResolutionVariable s₀ ⟨n, j⟩) =
      (finiteTateInclusion (finiteResolutionBase s₀) n).hom
        (finiteTateCycle (finiteResolutionBase s₀) n j) :=
    finiteTateColimitDifferential_variable (finiteResolutionBase s₀) n j
  rw [hD]
  match n with
  | 0 =>
      refine (finiteResolutionAugmentation_base φ s₀
        (finiteTateCycle (finiteResolutionBase s₀) 0 j)).trans ?_
      exact hs₀ _ j.2
  | m + 1 =>
      refine finiteResolutionAugmentation_positive φ s₀ (Nat.succ_ne_zero m) ?_
      exact (finiteTateInclusion (finiteResolutionBase s₀) (m + 1)).map_grade (m + 1) _
        (finiteTateCycle_mem (finiteResolutionBase s₀) (m + 1) j)

/-- Only one homogeneous component of a homogeneous element is nonzero, so the augmentation kills
the boundary of each component of a homogeneous element as soon as it kills the boundary of the
element itself. -/
public theorem finiteResolutionAugmentation_differential_gradedPart_of_mem_grade {k : ℕ}
    {x : (finiteResolutionAlgebra s₀).carrier}
    (hx : x ∈ (finiteResolutionAlgebra s₀).grade k)
    (h : (finiteResolutionAugmentation φ s₀).hom (finiteResolutionDifferential s₀ x) = 0)
    (m : ℕ) :
    (finiteResolutionAugmentation φ s₀).hom (finiteResolutionDifferential s₀
      (gradedPart (finiteResolutionAlgebra s₀).grade x m)) = 0 := by
  by_cases hm : k = m
  · subst hm
    rw [gradedPart_of_mem_same _ hx]
    exact h
  · rw [gradedPart_of_mem_ne _ hx hm, map_zero, map_zero]

/-- **The elements all of whose homogeneous components have their boundary killed by the
augmentation form a subring.** Closure under multiplication is the Leibniz rule: the boundary of a
product of two homogeneous elements is the sum of two terms, each of which has a factor that the
augmentation kills by hypothesis. The sign of the second term is harmless, the augmentation being
an `R`-algebra morphism.

The condition is imposed on every homogeneous component rather than on the element itself, because
the Leibniz rule relates homogeneous elements only, and a graded derivation of a product of two
inhomogeneous elements mixes the components. -/
public noncomputable def finiteResolutionBoundaryKernel :
    Subring (finiteResolutionAlgebra s₀).carrier where
  carrier := {x | ∀ m : ℕ, (finiteResolutionAugmentation φ s₀).hom
    (finiteResolutionDifferential s₀
      (gradedPart (finiteResolutionAlgebra s₀).grade x m)) = 0}
  zero_mem' := by
    intro m
    rw [gradedPart_zero, map_zero, map_zero]
  one_mem' := by
    refine finiteResolutionAugmentation_differential_gradedPart_of_mem_grade φ s₀
      (SetLike.one_mem_graded (finiteResolutionAlgebra s₀).grade) ?_
    rw [finiteResolutionDifferential_grade_zero s₀
      (SetLike.one_mem_graded (finiteResolutionAlgebra s₀).grade), map_zero]
  add_mem' := by
    intro x y hx hy m
    rw [gradedPart_add, map_add, map_add, hx m, hy m, add_zero]
  neg_mem' := by
    intro x hx m
    rw [gradedPart_neg, map_neg, map_neg, hx m, neg_zero]
  mul_mem' := by
    intro x y hx hy m
    obtain ⟨s, hs⟩ :=
      exists_gradedPart_decomposition (finiteResolutionAlgebra s₀).grade x
    obtain ⟨t, ht⟩ :=
      exists_gradedPart_decomposition (finiteResolutionAlgebra s₀).grade y
    have hxy : gradedPart (finiteResolutionAlgebra s₀).grade (x * y) m =
        ∑ i ∈ s, ∑ k ∈ t, gradedPart (finiteResolutionAlgebra s₀).grade
          (gradedPart (finiteResolutionAlgebra s₀).grade x i *
            gradedPart (finiteResolutionAlgebra s₀).grade y k) m := by
      conv_lhs => rw [hs, ht]
      exact gradedPart_mul_sum (finiteResolutionAlgebra s₀).grade _ _ m
    rw [hxy, map_sum, map_sum]
    refine Finset.sum_eq_zero fun i _ => ?_
    rw [map_sum, map_sum]
    refine Finset.sum_eq_zero fun k _ => ?_
    by_cases hik : i + k = m
    · rw [gradedPart_mul_homogeneous _ (gradedPart_mem _ x i) (gradedPart_mem _ y k) hik,
        finiteResolutionDifferential_leibniz s₀ (gradedPart_mem _ x i)
          (gradedPart_mem _ y k),
        map_add, map_mul, hx i, zero_mul, zero_add, map_smul, map_mul, hy k, mul_zero,
        smul_zero]
    · rw [gradedPart_mul_homogeneous_of_ne _ (gradedPart_mem _ x i) (gradedPart_mem _ y k) hik,
        map_zero, map_zero]

/-- A homogeneous element whose boundary the augmentation kills lies in the subring. -/
public theorem mem_finiteResolutionBoundaryKernel_of_mem_grade {k : ℕ}
    {x : (finiteResolutionAlgebra s₀).carrier}
    (hx : x ∈ (finiteResolutionAlgebra s₀).grade k)
    (h : (finiteResolutionAugmentation φ s₀).hom (finiteResolutionDifferential s₀ x) = 0) :
    x ∈ finiteResolutionBoundaryKernel φ s₀ :=
  finiteResolutionAugmentation_differential_gradedPart_of_mem_grade φ s₀ hx h

/-- The degree-zero polynomial algebra lies in the subring: its elements have degree zero, where
the differential vanishes. -/
public theorem finiteResolutionCoefficient_mem_finiteResolutionBoundaryKernel
    (p : MvPolynomial σ R) :
    finiteResolutionCoefficient s₀ p ∈ finiteResolutionBoundaryKernel φ s₀ := by
  refine mem_finiteResolutionBoundaryKernel_of_mem_grade φ s₀
    (finiteResolutionCoefficient_mem_grade_zero s₀ p) ?_
  rw [finiteResolutionDifferential_grade_zero s₀
    (finiteResolutionCoefficient_mem_grade_zero s₀ p), map_zero]

include hs₀ in
/-- Every variable lies in the subring. -/
public theorem finiteResolutionVariable_mem_finiteResolutionBoundaryKernel
    (j : Σ n : ℕ, finiteTateIndex (finiteResolutionBase s₀) n) :
    finiteResolutionVariable s₀ j ∈ finiteResolutionBoundaryKernel φ s₀ :=
  mem_finiteResolutionBoundaryKernel_of_mem_grade φ s₀ (finiteResolutionVariable_mem s₀ j)
    (finiteResolutionAugmentation_differential_variable φ s₀ hs₀ j)

include hs₀ in
/-- **Every divided power of a variable lies in the subring.** A divided power of a variable is
homogeneous, and by compatibility of the divided powers with the differential its boundary has the
boundary of the variable as a factor. -/
public theorem dpow_finiteResolutionVariable_mem_finiteResolutionBoundaryKernel
    (j : Σ n : ℕ, finiteTateIndex (finiteResolutionBase s₀) n) (n : ℕ) :
    (finiteResolutionAlgebra s₀).gamma.dpow n (finiteResolutionVariable s₀ j) ∈
      finiteResolutionBoundaryKernel φ s₀ := by
  by_cases hpos : finiteResolutionVariable s₀ j ∈
      positiveEvenPart (finiteResolutionAlgebra s₀).grade
  · match n with
    | 0 =>
        rw [(finiteResolutionAlgebra s₀).gamma.dpow_zero hpos]
        exact one_mem _
    | n + 1 =>
        have htail : (finiteResolutionAugmentation φ s₀).hom
            (finiteResolutionDifferential s₀
              ((finiteResolutionAlgebra s₀).gamma.dpow (n + 1)
                (finiteResolutionVariable s₀ j))) = 0 := by
          rw [(finiteTateColimitCompatible (finiteResolutionBase s₀)).d_dpow
              (Nat.succ_ne_zero n) hpos, map_mul,
            finiteResolutionAugmentation_differential_variable φ s₀ hs₀ j, zero_mul]
        rcases positiveEven_homogeneous_eq_zero_or_grade (finiteResolutionAlgebra s₀).grade
          (finiteResolutionVariable_mem s₀ j) hpos with hzero | ⟨d, hd⟩
        · refine mem_finiteResolutionBoundaryKernel_of_mem_grade φ s₀ (k := 0) ?_ htail
          have hmem := (finiteResolutionAlgebra s₀).gamma.dpow_mem_grade (d := 0)
            (Nat.succ_ne_zero n) hpos (by rw [hzero]; exact Submodule.zero_mem _)
          simpa using hmem
        · refine mem_finiteResolutionBoundaryKernel_of_mem_grade φ s₀
            (k := 2 * (n + 1) * (d + 1)) ?_ htail
          exact (finiteResolutionAlgebra s₀).gamma.dpow_mem_grade (Nat.succ_ne_zero n) hpos
            (hd ▸ finiteResolutionVariable_mem s₀ j)
  · rw [(finiteResolutionAlgebra s₀).gamma.dpow_null hpos]
    exact zero_mem _

include hs₀ in
/-- **The augmentation kills every boundary.** The elements whose homogeneous components have their
boundaries killed form a subring containing the coefficients, the variables and all their divided
powers, hence containing every finite variable stage; the finite variable stages cover the
resolution, so the condition holds for every element, and summing it over the homogeneous
components of an element gives the statement for that element.

This is the one clause of Tag 09PN that is not degree bookkeeping: a boundary coming from degree
one is a degree-zero element, where the augmentation is `φ` itself. What makes it vanish is that
the boundary of a variable of degree one is a member of the prescribed generating family of
`ker φ`. -/
public theorem finiteResolutionAugmentation_differential
    (x : (finiteResolutionAlgebra s₀).carrier) :
    (finiteResolutionAugmentation φ s₀).hom (finiteResolutionDifferential s₀ x) = 0 := by
  obtain ⟨T, hT⟩ := finiteResolutionAlgebra_finite_support s₀ x
  have hx : x ∈ finiteResolutionBoundaryKernel φ s₀ :=
    finiteVariableStage_map_mem_subring (finiteResolutionAlgebra s₀).gamma
      (finiteResolutionCoefficient s₀) (finiteResolutionVariable s₀) T
      (AlgHom.id R (finiteResolutionAlgebra s₀).carrier)
      (finiteResolutionBoundaryKernel φ s₀)
      (fun p => finiteResolutionCoefficient_mem_finiteResolutionBoundaryKernel φ s₀ p)
      (fun j _ => finiteResolutionVariable_mem_finiteResolutionBoundaryKernel φ s₀ hs₀ j)
      (fun j _ n =>
        dpow_finiteResolutionVariable_mem_finiteResolutionBoundaryKernel φ s₀ hs₀ j n) hT
  obtain ⟨s, hs⟩ := exists_gradedPart_decomposition (finiteResolutionAlgebra s₀).grade x
  conv_lhs => rw [hs]
  rw [map_sum, map_sum]
  exact Finset.sum_eq_zero fun m _ => hx m

end Augmentation

section Existence

variable {R : Type u} {S : Type v} {σ : Type w} [CommRing R] [CommRing S] [Algebra R S]

/-- Each member of a generating family of the kernel of the presentation is killed by it. -/
public theorem resolutionPresentation_eq_zero_of_mem (φ : MvPolynomial σ R →ₐ[R] S)
    (s₀ : Finset (resolutionCoefficientRing R S σ))
    (hs₀ : Ideal.span (↑s₀ : Set (resolutionCoefficientRing R S σ)) =
      RingHom.ker (resolutionPresentation φ)) :
    ∀ p ∈ s₀, resolutionPresentation φ p = 0 := fun _ hp =>
  RingHom.mem_ker.1 (hs₀.le (Ideal.subset_span (Finset.mem_coe.2 hp)))

/-- **Tate's resolution with finitely many generators in each degree, for a prescribed
presentation.** Let `φ : R[xᵢ] → S` be a surjection from a polynomial ring whose coefficient ring
is Noetherian, and let `s₀` be a finite generating family of `ker φ`. Then `φ` extends to a divided
power differential graded algebra over `R`, free over `R` in every degree, which is a graded
divided power polynomial algebra over `R[xᵢ]` on **finitely many variables in each degree**, and
whose augmentation to `S` is a quasi-isomorphism.

The algebra is the colimit of the degreewise finite tower over `R[xᵢ]` with `s₀` as the family of
degree-zero cycles to be killed. Exactness in degree zero is `s₀` generating `ker φ`; exactness in
positive degrees is that the finite family killed at each stage spans the cycles of its degree,
which is `DividedPowers.TateResolution.hasFiniteSpanningCycles_finiteTateStage`. The finiteness of
the index sets is `DividedPowers.TateResolution.finite_finiteTateIndex`. -/
@[stacks 09PN "finite generators in each degree, for a prescribed presentation"]
public noncomputable def finiteTateResolution (φ : MvPolynomial σ R →ₐ[R] S)
    (hφ : Function.Surjective φ) (s₀ : Finset (resolutionCoefficientRing R S σ))
    (hs₀ : Ideal.span (↑s₀ : Set (resolutionCoefficientRing R S σ)) =
      RingHom.ker (resolutionPresentation φ))
    [IsNoetherianRing (resolutionCoefficientRing R S σ)] :
    Resolution R S σ φ where
  A := (finiteResolutionAlgebra s₀).carrier
  ring := (finiteResolutionAlgebra s₀).ring
  algebra := (finiteResolutionAlgebra s₀).algebra
  grade := (finiteResolutionAlgebra s₀).grade
  graded := (finiteResolutionAlgebra s₀).graded
  free := fun n => (finiteResolutionPolynomial s₀).free_grade n
  strict := (finiteResolutionAlgebra s₀).strict
  gamma := (finiteResolutionAlgebra s₀).gamma
  differential := finiteResolutionDifferential s₀
  dga := finiteTateColimitDga (finiteResolutionBase s₀)
  compatible := finiteTateColimitCompatible (finiteResolutionBase s₀)
  coefficient := finiteResolutionCoefficient s₀
  augmentation := (finiteResolutionAugmentation φ s₀).hom
  polynomial := finiteResolutionPolynomial s₀
  augmentation_surjective := by
    intro s
    obtain ⟨p, hp⟩ := hφ s
    exact ⟨finiteResolutionCoefficient s₀ p,
      (finiteResolutionAugmentation_coefficient φ s₀ p).trans hp⟩
  degree_zero := mem_finiteResolutionAlgebra_grade_zero_iff s₀
  augmentation_coefficient := AlgHom.ext (finiteResolutionAugmentation_coefficient φ s₀)
  augmentation_positive := fun n a hn ha =>
    finiteResolutionAugmentation_positive φ s₀ (Nat.pos_iff_ne_zero.1 hn) ha
  augmentation_differential := finiteResolutionAugmentation_differential φ s₀
    (resolutionPresentation_eq_zero_of_mem φ s₀ hs₀)
  exact_zero := by
    intro a ha haug
    obtain ⟨p, hp⟩ := (mem_finiteResolutionAlgebra_grade_zero_iff s₀ a).1 ha
    have hker : resolutionPresentation φ ((resolutionCoefficientEquiv R S σ).symm p) = 0 := by
      refine (finiteResolutionAugmentation_base φ s₀ _).symm.trans ?_
      rw [← hp] at haug
      exact haug
    obtain ⟨c, -, hc⟩ := Submodule.mem_span_finset.1 (hs₀.ge (RingHom.mem_ker.2 hker))
    have hc' : ∑ y ∈ s₀, c y * y = (resolutionCoefficientEquiv R S σ).symm p := by
      simpa only [smul_eq_mul] using hc
    obtain ⟨b, hb, hdb⟩ :=
      exists_finiteTateColimitDifferential_boundary_of_base.{u, max v w} (R := R)
        (resolutionCoefficientRing R S σ) s₀ c
    exact ⟨b, hb, hdb.trans (Eq.trans (congrArg
      (fun z : resolutionCoefficientRing R S σ =>
        (finiteTateCoefficient (finiteResolutionBase s₀)).hom z) hc') hp)⟩
  exact_positive := by
    intro n a hn ha hda
    obtain ⟨m, rfl⟩ := Nat.exists_eq_succ_of_ne_zero (Nat.pos_iff_ne_zero.1 hn)
    exact exists_finiteTateColimitDifferential_boundary_of_cycle (finiteResolutionBase s₀)
      (fun k => hasFiniteSpanningCycles_finiteTateStage.{u, max v w} (R := R)
        (resolutionCoefficientRing R S σ) s₀ (k + 1) (k + 1)) m ha hda

/-- The degreewise finite Tate resolution really is degreewise finite: it has finitely many
variables in each degree, which is the finiteness assertion of Tag 09PN. The variables of degree
`i + 1` are the family chosen at the stage `i` of the tower, which is finite by construction. -/
@[stacks 09PN "finite generators in each degree, for a prescribed presentation"]
public theorem finite_finiteTateResolution_polynomialIndices (φ : MvPolynomial σ R →ₐ[R] S)
    (hφ : Function.Surjective φ) (s₀ : Finset (resolutionCoefficientRing R S σ))
    (hs₀ : Ideal.span (↑s₀ : Set (resolutionCoefficientRing R S σ)) =
      RingHom.ker (resolutionPresentation φ))
    [IsNoetherianRing (resolutionCoefficientRing R S σ)] (i : ℕ) :
    Finite ((finiteTateResolution φ hφ s₀ hs₀).polynomialIndices i) :=
  finite_finiteTateIndex (finiteResolutionBase s₀)
    (finite_finiteTateBase_kill (resolutionCoefficientRing R S σ) s₀) i

end Existence

section Noetherian

/-- **The finiteness half of Tag 09PN.** A Noetherian commutative ring `R` and an `R`-algebra `S`
of finite type admit a surjection `φ : R[x₁, …, x_k] → S` from a polynomial ring in finitely many
variables together with a Tate resolution of `S` along `φ` having **finitely many generators in
each degree**.

The presentation is a finite one, which exists because `S` is of finite type; its coefficient ring
`R[x₁, …, x_k]` is Noetherian because `R` is, by `MvPolynomial.isNoetherianRing`, so its kernel is
generated by a finite family, and
`DividedPowers.TateResolution.finiteTateResolution` resolves that data. -/
@[stacks 09PN "finite generators in each degree"]
public theorem exists_finite_tateResolution (R S : Type u) [CommRing R] [CommRing S]
    [Algebra R S] [IsNoetherianRing R] [Algebra.FiniteType R S] :
    ∃ (k : ℕ) (φ : MvPolynomial (Fin k) R →ₐ[R] S) (A : Resolution R S (Fin k) φ),
      Function.Surjective φ ∧ ∀ i : ℕ, Finite (A.polynomialIndices i) := by
  classical
  have hft : Algebra.FiniteType R S := inferInstance
  obtain ⟨k, φ, hφ⟩ := Algebra.FiniteType.iff_quotient_mvPolynomial''.1 hft
  have : IsNoetherianRing (resolutionCoefficientRing R S (Fin k)) :=
    isNoetherianRing_of_ringEquiv (MvPolynomial (Fin k) R) ULift.ringEquiv.symm
  obtain ⟨s₀, hs₀⟩ :=
    IsNoetherian.noetherian (RingHom.ker (resolutionPresentation φ) : Ideal _)
  exact ⟨k, φ, finiteTateResolution φ hφ s₀ hs₀, hφ,
    fun i => finite_finiteTateResolution_polynomialIndices φ hφ s₀ hs₀ i⟩

end Noetherian

end DividedPowers.TateResolution

